<section class="volume">

    <div class="container">

        <div class="volume__grid">

            <div class="volume__content">

                <div class="volume__eyebrow">
                    <span>06</span>
                    Why volume beats a checklist
                </div>

                <h2 class="volume__title">
                    You run one project. We've run twelve, at once.
                </h2>

                <div class="volume__text">

                    <p>
                        A developer usually has one project running — a large hotel, say, or a villa complex — and leans on an in-house buyer sourcing furniture from scratch for it, against a deadline. We've run twelve projects at once, against the same 450+ factories, on top of 38 completed since we started. That's not a longer supplier list, it's thousands more real purchase cycles than any single procurement team will run in a career: enough to know which factory holds its tolerances and which one quietly substitutes materials when nobody's checking. It also buys a rate no single project can negotiate alone — a factory prices someone ordering weekly, year-round, differently than someone ordering once.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That knowledge doesn't stop compounding when a project ends. We stay inside the same network as buyers running 20-30 years of procurement on these factory floors, and when a developer's project wraps — with us or elsewhere — that experience feeds back into what we check the next factory against. The list of 450+ factories isn't static: it gets more accurate every month because the market keeps talking to us, and we keep testing what it says against our own results.
                    </p>

                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="volume__stat">

                <div class="volume__number">12</div>

                <div class="volume__label">
                    Projects we've run in parallel,<br />at once
                </div>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>

</section>

